// lanekeep's host API, as the component model sees it.
//
// This file is the single source of truth for the boundary between the engine and a
// *component* rule. `src/bindings.rs` generates the Rust bindings from it with
// `wasmtime::component::bindgen!`, and the engine implements exactly what it declares.
//
// It is also the single source of truth for the *other* engine's boundary. The definitions a
// TypeScript rule author installs, `packages/lanekeep/index.d.ts`, are **generated** from this
// world by `crates/lanekeep-types-gen`, which maps the `check-context` / `reduce-context` /
// `binding-kind` declarations onto the QuickJS-shaped surface the sandbox exposes
// (`report`/`emitFact` folding parameters into an object, `facts` on the per-file context, and
// so on). What holds that mapping honest is `crates/lanekeep-types-gen/tests/generated.rs`,
// which asserts the committed file is exactly the renderer's output — there is no second,
// hand-maintained description to drift.
//
// Its bytes are a cache-key input — `host_api_hash` is blake3 over this source, taken by
// `src/key.rs` — so an edit here, including to a comment, invalidates every cached result.
// That is the point: the `0.1.0` in the package declaration below is for error messages and
// for the component model's conventions, and is deliberately not what the cache keys on,
// because a hand-maintained version is a thing somebody has to remember to bump.
//
// Adding a function widens the trust boundary described in `docs/architecture.md` §13: a
// component reaches exactly what its world declares and nothing else, which is why the
// absence of `fs`, `process`, network and the clock is structural here rather than curated.
package lanekeep:host@0.1.0;

/// The whole host surface: the data types that cross the boundary, and the two contexts
/// that carry the functions.
///
/// One interface, not one per phase, and that was decided by building both.
///
/// The alternative on the table was three interfaces — `types`, `check-api`, `reduce-api` —
/// on the theory that a per-file rule could then not so much as *name* the cross-file
/// context. Both variants were written out in full and the same guest built against each on
/// 2026-08-05, `wasm-tools` 1.255.0, `cargo-component` 0.21.1, `wit-bindgen` 0.41.0,
/// `rustc` 1.95.0, `wasm32-unknown-unknown`:
///
/// | | artifact imports | bytes | guest bindings name both contexts |
/// |---|---|---|---|
/// | one interface | 1 | 26,644 | yes |
/// | three interfaces | 3 | 26,979 | yes |
///
/// **The split does not deliver what it was proposed for.** The `rule` world exports both
/// `check` and `reduce`, so it must import both phases' interfaces to give those two
/// exports their argument types; `wit-parser` then adds `import types` on its own, and the
/// split guest's bindings open with `pub type CheckContext = ...` and
/// `pub type ReduceContext = ...` exactly as the single-interface guest's do. Literal
/// unnameability needs two *worlds*, which doubles the build artifact for every rule that
/// has a reduce phase — and that trade was already rejected. So the split costs three
/// imports where one will do, and buys nothing.
///
/// The residual weakness is therefore real and is stated plainly rather than described as
/// impossible: a per-file rule can name `reduce-context`, and the generated bindings hand
/// it a `pub unsafe fn from_handle(handle: u32)` with which to forge one. What it cannot do
/// is obtain a *valid* one. Two independent layers stop it. The method sets are disjoint,
/// so a wrong-phase call does not compile. And a forged handle traps in the runtime with
/// "unknown handle index", because the instance's table for the other phase's resource is
/// empty during this call — the host is never reached, and it records that it was never
/// reached. That second layer is the load-bearing one, since a compile error only guards a
/// rule someone compiles from source and says nothing about a component that arrives
/// prebuilt.
///
/// **Both layers are now measured against this world rather than against the prototype, and
/// in both directions.** `tests/fixtures/reduce/` forges the other phase's handle from each
/// phase, with the bindings' own `unsafe fn from_handle(0)` — zero being the handle the call
/// already holds for its own phase, which is the most plausible forgery available. Measured
/// 2026-08-06, `wasmtime` 47.0.3, `cargo-component` 0.21.1, `rustc` 1.95.0: the trap's root
/// cause is exactly `unknown handle index 0`, the other phase's host state records nothing,
/// and the guest's own announcement immediately before the forgery *is* recorded — so the
/// call died at the forgery and not earlier. The compile-time layer was confirmed by hand in
/// the same fixture: `ctx.files()` inside `check` fails with ``error[E0599]: no method named
/// `files` found for reference `&types::CheckContext` in the current scope``. Both are
/// described in `tests/reduce.rs`, which owns the runtime half; the declaration half — that
/// the two blocks below share exactly one method name — is asserted against this file in
/// `tests/facts.rs`.
interface types {
  /// An opaque reference into the arena of the check-context that produced it. Meaningless
  /// outside the call that handed it out — the same discipline the published `Node` type
  /// already enforces in TypeScript, restated in a form every target language shares.
  ///
  /// The root of a parsed file is handle 0. Compare against a sentinel explicitly, never
  /// test truthiness: `if (!node)` discards the root, silently, and only ever by removing
  /// results. This is why `closest-ancestor` returns `option<match>` rather than an empty
  /// list.
  type node = u32;

  /// Where a per-file violation is reported. Every field is known, because a `node` always
  /// has a position in the file it came from.
  record node-location {
    file: string,
    line: u32,
    column: u32,
  }

  /// Where a cross-file violation is reported. The reduce phase never touches parse trees
  /// (`docs/architecture.md`'s reduce invariant), so it has no node to take a position from
  /// and must carry one captured earlier — on a fact, during the per-file pass, while the
  /// tree was still there.
  ///
  /// **`line` and `column` are optional in this record and required by the host.** The option
  /// mirrors the published TypeScript `ReduceLocation`, where both have always been optional
  /// in the type and required by the runtime; it is not permission to omit them. A `report`
  /// naming no site fails the call, in both engines, because the only stand-in available
  /// downstream is 1:1 — which points a reader at an unrelated line and cannot be told apart
  /// from a rule that meant 1:1. A rule that means "the whole file" says `1, 1` itself.
  ///
  /// Declaring them `u32` would make that structural rather than enforced, and is the stronger
  /// shape. It is not taken here because it would put this record out of step with the
  /// published type it mirrors, and because it is an ABI change. That second reason used to
  /// carry a window with it — cheap only while no authoring crate binds against this world —
  /// and the window has closed: `rust-rules/no-unwrap` and `rust-rules/no-glob-import` both
  /// point `[package.metadata.component.target]` at this directory, and their artifacts are
  /// committed and hashed. Changing the record now invalidates every one of them and any
  /// component built outside this repository besides, which inverts what this paragraph used
  /// to recommend: the option stays, and the host keeps enforcing what the type will not.
  record reduce-location {
    file: string,
    line: option<u32>,
    column: option<u32>,
  }

  /// A suggested edit replacing a node's text.
  record fix {
    node: node,
    text: string,
    /// The rule's own claim that applying this preserves behavior, which the engine does not
    /// verify. `--fix` applies the safe ones and offers the rest as suggestions.
    ///
    /// Optional, and **the host decides what unspecified means: not safe.** The two mistakes
    /// are not symmetric — a suggestion that should have been safe costs a manual edit, and a
    /// fix that should have been a suggestion rewrites someone's code. A plain `bool` cannot
    /// represent "did not say", so every authoring crate would have to choose a value to send
    /// when its author omitted one, and one of them mapping that to `true` would silently
    /// make fixes auto-applicable with nothing here to catch it. `option<bool>` keeps the
    /// cautious answer an invariant of the boundary rather than a convention each language
    /// re-decides. This is what `lanekeep-js`'s `read_fix` already does with an absent
    /// property.
    safe: option<bool>,
  }

  /// One capture of a query match: the name the query gave it, and the node it bound.
  record match-entry {
    name: string,
    node: node,
  }

  /// A query match's captures.
  ///
  /// A list rather than a record, because a query's capture names are declared by the rule
  /// and are not known to this interface in advance, and WIT records have statically named
  /// fields. A capture that did not participate is simply absent. There is no WIT analog of
  /// a JavaScript object holding an explicit `undefined` under a key, so the ambiguity that
  /// distinction could cause does not exist here.
  type match = list<match-entry>;

  /// A fact emitted during the per-file pass, for the reduce phase to consume.
  record emitted-fact {
    kind: string,
    file: string,
    /// The rest of the payload, as the rule's own language serialized it to JSON. Opaque to
    /// the host beyond `kind` — a fact's shape is caller-defined. The host parses it and
    /// rejects what is not a JSON object: under QuickJS `JSON.stringify` guaranteed the
    /// shape, and a component can hand over any bytes it likes.
    data: string,
  }

  /// A short code snippet on each side of the rule: `bad` triggers it, `good` does not.
  record rule-examples {
    bad: string,
    good: string,
  }

  /// The three strings `lanekeep explain`, the agent reporter and context injection all
  /// read. Mandatory, together — a card with a message and no remediation tells an agent
  /// what is wrong and not what to do instead, which is the half that matters.
  record rule-card {
    message: string,
    remediation: string,
    examples: rule-examples,
  }

  /// Evaluated in Rust before the file is read or parsed. Purely an optimization — narrowing
  /// what gets read and parsed, never what a rule that ran would have reported.
  ///
  /// Mirrors `lanekeep_core::Gates` field for field. `path-matches` admits only a path
  /// matching one of these; `path-not-matches` is checked first and wins over it, skipping a
  /// path matching any of these even if `path-matches` would have admitted it. `file-contains`
  /// is an **and** — every listed substring must be present, so a rule matching either of two
  /// tokens cannot express it here and omits the gate instead — and `file-not-contains` is an
  /// **or**: a file whose bytes contain any of these is skipped.
  record rule-gates {
    path-matches: list<string>,
    path-not-matches: list<string>,
    file-contains: list<string>,
    file-not-contains: list<string>,
  }

  /// What a component says it is, read once at prepare time.
  ///
  /// A plain record returned by a function rather than a resource: it is read once and
  /// never again, and there is no per-call state for a resource to hold.
  ///
  /// The field set is exactly what `lanekeep-config`'s `EXTRACT` already harvests from a
  /// TypeScript module, and deliberately nothing more — a second, wider description of a rule
  /// is drift this record must not accumulate.
  record rule-metadata {
    id: string,
    /// One or several. A rule does not run on a file whose language it does not name — which
    /// means an empty list is not "every language," it is **no file at all**, and silently: a
    /// component that forgot to say what it targets would simply never fire, with nothing to
    /// report the mistake. The host refuses an empty list at load, before any file is checked.
    languages: list<string>,
    severity: string,
    card: rule-card,
    query: string,
    gates: rule-gates,
    /// Milliseconds. None means the default per-invocation budget.
    timeout: option<u64>,
  }

  /// How a name was introduced, as resolved by the language's binding resolution.
  ///
  /// Exactly two cases collide with a WIT keyword and are `%`-escaped for that reason
  /// alone; the escape carries no meaning. The rest are written bare because they are not
  /// keywords, which is measurable rather than assumed: `wasm-tools component wit` prints
  /// this file back with `%` on `import` and `type` and on nothing else, and `const`, `let`,
  /// `var`, `function`, `class`, `loop`, `module`, `trait` and `match` all parse unescaped.
  /// Escaping a non-keyword is legal, so an over-escaped version of this enum would have
  /// worked and would have said something false about WIT.
  enum binding-kind {
    %import, const, let, var, param, function, class, catch-param,
    assignment, loop, context-manager, comprehension,
    %type, receiver, type-param, module, trait,
  }

  /// Why a tracked read was refused. Each case carries the offending path.
  variant read-error {
    /// The path resolved outside the project root.
    escapes-root(string),
    /// The path was absolute, which a rule may not use — reads are root-relative so that a
    /// result does not depend on where the project sits on disk.
    absolute(string),
    /// The file exists but is not valid UTF-8.
    not-text(string),
  }

  /// Why a fact was refused.
  variant fact-error {
    /// `kind` was empty, so nothing could select the fact in the reduce phase.
    empty-kind,
    /// `data` parsed as JSON but was not an object.
    not-an-object,
    /// `data` did not parse as JSON. Carries the parser's message.
    invalid-json(string),
  }

  /// One frame of a guest's own stack, innermost first.
  ///
  /// Positions are in whatever space the guest was compiled to — for a bundled JavaScript
  /// rule that is the bundle, not the author's TypeScript. The host remaps them through the
  /// component's source map before a user sees one.
  record stack-frame {
    function: string,
    file: string,
    line: u32,
    column: u32,
  }

  /// Why a rule failed, when its language can say.
  ///
  /// A rule that fails *gracefully* returns one of these; a rule that traps still traps, and
  /// the host handles both. This exists because a guest with no error channel loses
  /// everything: measured 2026-08-10, an uncaught JavaScript throw inside a component
  /// reaches the host as `wasm trap: unreachable` with the message, the type and the stack
  /// all gone. A Rust guest that panics is that case and stays that case; `frames` is empty
  /// for a language with no stack to offer.
  record rule-error {
    message: string,
    frames: list<stack-frame>,
  }

  /// The per-file phase's capabilities, and the parse tree it is reading.
  ///
  /// Held as a resource rather than passed as a record so that its method set *is* the
  /// enforcement `docs/architecture.md` §6.5 asks for: a per-file rule is handed one of
  /// these and nothing else, and there is no exported constructor, so it cannot make one of
  /// the other kind.
  resource check-context {
    /// The path of the file being checked, relative to the project root.
    file-path: func() -> string;
    /// The full source text of the file being checked.
    file-text: func() -> string;
    /// The root node of the parsed file. Its handle is 0.
    root: func() -> node;

    /// The node's grammar kind, or none if the handle is not live in this context.
    kind: func(n: node) -> option<string>;
    /// The source text the node spans.
    text: func(n: node) -> option<string>;
    /// Whether the node is named in the grammar, as opposed to an anonymous token.
    is-named: func(n: node) -> bool;
    /// The node's one-based start line.
    ///
    /// One-based, matching every other position lanekeep reports: `node-location` below,
    /// the violations the engine sorts by `(ruleId, file, line, column)`, and the column
    /// `lanekeep-query` builds by adding one to tree-sitter's own zero-based figure. Both
    /// engines read the same `NodeArena::position`, so there is one answer rather than a
    /// convention per boundary — a zero-based reading here would put every component rule's
    /// violations one line and one column off what the identical TypeScript rule reports,
    /// which looks like a rule bug and is not one.
    line: func(n: node) -> option<u32>;
    /// The node's one-based start column, on the same footing as `line`.
    column: func(n: node) -> option<u32>;
    /// The node's parent, or none at the root.
    parent: func(n: node) -> option<node>;
    /// Every child, named and anonymous, in tree order.
    children: func(n: node) -> list<node>;
    /// The named children only, in tree order.
    named-children: func(n: node) -> list<node>;
    /// Every ancestor, nearest first, ending at the root.
    ancestors: func(n: node) -> list<node>;

    /// Whether the identifier at `n` resolves to a binding introduced by importing `name`
    /// (or the module itself, when `name` is none) from `module`.
    resolves-to-import: func(n: node, module: string, name: option<string>) -> bool;
    /// Whether the identifier at `n` resolves to a binding imported from a module matching
    /// the glob `pattern`.
    is-imported-from: func(n: node, pattern: string) -> bool;
    /// How the binding the identifier at `n` resolves to was introduced.
    binding-kind: func(n: node) -> option<binding-kind>;
    /// Whether the binding the identifier at `n` resolves to shadows an outer one.
    is-shadowed: func(n: node) -> bool;

    /// Run a query against the subtree rooted at `n`. The error is the query compiler's
    /// message.
    query-subtree: func(n: node, query: string) -> result<list<match>, string>;
    /// The nearest ancestor of `n` matching the query, or none.
    ///
    /// `option<match>` rather than an empty list, deliberately: an empty object is truthy
    /// in JavaScript, so a "nothing matched" value shaped like a match takes the wrong
    /// branch under `if (!...)`. The error is the query compiler's message.
    closest-ancestor: func(n: node, query: string) -> result<option<match>, string>;

    /// Read another file in the project, root-relative. The read is tracked: it becomes a
    /// dependency of this file's cached result. None means the file does not exist.
    read-file: func(path: string) -> result<option<string>, read-error>;
    /// Whether another file exists, root-relative. Tracked exactly as `read-file` is.
    file-exists: func(path: string) -> result<bool, read-error>;

    /// Emit a fact for the reduce phase. `data` must be a JSON object.
    emit-fact: func(kind: string, data: string) -> result<_, fact-error>;

    /// The node's position, for a rule that needs to reason about it rather than report at
    /// it.
    loc: func(n: node) -> option<node-location>;
    /// Report a violation at `n`.
    ///
    /// Two independently optional named parameters rather than one union argument: the
    /// union exists in the TypeScript API only because JavaScript has no optional named
    /// parameters, and WIT does.
    report: func(n: node, message: option<string>, fix: option<fix>);

    /// Today's date, as `YYYY-MM-DD`, or none when the rule is not permitted to observe it.
    ///
    /// A function, not a property. Every capability here is already a host-executed call,
    /// so "was the date read" is "did this implementation run" — the cache needs that
    /// answer, and there is no plain-data-property alternative it has to be distinguished
    /// from.
    today: func() -> option<string>;
  }

  /// The cross-file phase's capabilities.
  ///
  /// No node, no tree, no file text: the reduce phase consumes facts and the file list and
  /// nothing else, which is what keeps cross-file rules parallel and cacheable
  /// (`docs/architecture.md`'s reduce invariant). The absence is the point, and here it is
  /// structural — there is no method to decline.
  resource reduce-context {
    /// Every file the run considered, in the engine's deterministic order.
    files: func() -> list<string>;
    /// The facts emitted during the per-file pass, optionally narrowed to one kind.
    facts: func(kind: option<string>) -> list<emitted-fact>;
    /// Report a cross-file violation.
    report: func(at: reduce-location, message: option<string>);
  }
}

/// What a rule component is today: seven exports over the host surface, for the one or more
/// rules the component hosts.
///
/// **A component hosts a *list* of rules, and every export but `rules` takes an index into
/// it.** That was forced rather than chosen. One embedded JavaScript engine is 12.34 MiB
/// measured, and the rules on top of it are noise — three additional rules with real bodies
/// cost 9,702 bytes — so one component per rule would ship four migrated built-ins as 49.37
/// MiB where one shared component is 12.34 MiB. The shape also buys something for the target
/// that did not need it: one Rust crate can ship a family of related rules in one 26 KB
/// artifact. A rule authored alone is a ruleset of one, and pays a `u32` for it.
///
/// **This list is still provisional, and the version number is where that shows.**
/// `metadata` carries what a TypeScript rule's default export carries today (id, languages,
/// severity, card, gates, query, timeout), read once at prepare time. `configure` is what a
/// sub-project binding against this world needed and this file did not yet have: a call made
/// once after instantiation, since a component cannot close over a host-supplied value the
/// way a factory rule does in JavaScript, so a rule's options cross as JSON data instead —
/// see `configure`'s own doc comment below for why that is a permanent expressiveness loss
/// against a factory rather than a gap to design around.
///
/// **Adding an export bumps `host_api_hash`, which is a cache-key input**, so every cached
/// result in every checkout is invalidated by it — which is the right behavior, since a
/// result computed against a world without the addition is not a valid result for a run that
/// has it. `metadata` paid that cost once, `configure` paid it again, and this change pays it
/// a third time while also changing five signatures.
///
/// **The rules of one component share an instance, and therefore share every global the guest
/// keeps.** The host instantiates once per (worker, component) rather than once per rule —
/// resident bytes are not artifact bytes: an instance grants 9,502,720 bytes before it runs,
/// and one store takes seven of them and refuses the eighth with `MemoryExceeded`, so four is
/// not a breach — what it costs is headroom, taking what is left for a rule's own allocations
/// from 54.9 MiB to 27.8 MiB. So a `static mut`, a lazily
/// built table, a memo: all of them are visible to every rule this component hosts, and *when*
/// each rule sees them depends on the order the host's work-stealing scheduler handed a worker
/// its files. `configure` is the only per-index state the arrangement guarantees.
///
/// The rule that keeps this sound: **state outliving a `check` call must be derivable from
/// that call's inputs.** A memo keyed on the file path is fine, because being handed a
/// populated one is indistinguishable from being handed an empty one. A counter of files seen
/// is not, and neither is anything `reduce` reads that `check` wrote to a variable rather than
/// emitting as a fact — facts are the hand-off between the two phases and are per file by
/// construction. A rule breaking this reports different violations depending on scheduling,
/// which is the nondeterminism the whole design exists to make impossible.
world rule {
  import types;

  /// A type declared inside an interface is not in scope in the world that imports it. The
  /// world needs an explicit `use` for every type it names in an export signature, or it
  /// does not parse.
  ///
  /// Measured on this file, 2026-08-05, `wasm-tools` 1.255.0: with the `use` line deleted
  /// the error is ``name `check-context` does not exist``, pointing at `export check`'s
  /// parameter. It names whichever type the first offending export signature mentions, and
  /// it reads as though the type is missing rather than out of scope — the type is right
  /// there in `types`, twenty lines up.
  ///
  /// This list is what a guest gets at the top level of its bindings; everything else in
  /// `types` is reached through the interface path. `check-context`, `reduce-context`,
  /// `match`, `rule-metadata` and `rule-error` are here because the exports below name them.
  /// `node` is not
  /// named below and is here anyway — for a Rust guest that buys nothing, measured:
  /// `wit-bindgen` 0.41.0 resolves `type node = u32` to `u32` and emits no alias, so the top
  /// of the generated bindings is the same four names with or without it, and the whole cost
  /// is 23 bytes of artifact metadata. It stays because languages that render an alias for a
  /// primitive typedef would put `node` where rule authors expect it, and 23 bytes is not a
  /// reason to find out the hard way which ones those are.
  ///
  /// `stack-frame` is deliberately absent: it appears only inside `rule-error` rather than in
  /// an export signature, which is the same reason `reduce-location` is not here.
  use types.{node, check-context, reduce-context, match, rule-metadata, rule-error};

  /// Every rule this component hosts, by id, in index order. The index is the `rule`
  /// parameter every other export takes.
  ///
  /// Ids rather than full metadata, and the split is load-bearing. `configure` must run
  /// before metadata is read, because a factory rule's card and query are produced by
  /// applying the factory to its options. But to configure rule *i* you must know *i*
  /// exists. A rule's id cannot depend on its options — the id is how a config names the
  /// rule — so ids enumerate before configuration and everything else is read after it.
  export rules: func() -> list<string>;

  /// What one of this component's rules is. Read once, at prepare time, before any file is
  /// checked, and after that rule has been configured.
  export metadata: func(rule: u32) -> rule-metadata;

  /// Hand one of this component's rules its configured options, once, after instantiation
  /// and before any `check`.
  ///
  /// JSON rather than a typed record, because a rule's options are its own design and this
  /// interface cannot know their shape. A component has no way to close over a host-supplied
  /// value the way a JavaScript factory does, so the value crosses as data — which is a
  /// permanent expressiveness loss against a factory, named rather than designed around: no
  /// functions, no regular expressions, nothing a `Deserialize` cannot represent.
  ///
  /// `null` when a rule was named with no options, so a guest has one code path rather than
  /// two. The error is the guest's own message, and it fails the run rather than trapping —
  /// a rule that cannot use its configuration has not merely misbehaved, it has been
  /// misconfigured, and the user needs to be told which.
  export configure: func(rule: u32, options-json: string) -> result<_, string>;

  /// Whether this rule has a per-file pass.
  ///
  /// **Probed once per rule at config load, and the answer is what the engine dispatches on.**
  /// `lanekeep-config` calls this and `has-reduce` immediately after `metadata`, and folds all
  /// three into the rule's spec — so a component describes its own passes and is no longer
  /// taken at its config's word. This mirrors how the presence of `check` is discovered for a
  /// TypeScript rule, by inspecting the evaluated module rather than by trusting hand-written
  /// config.
  ///
  /// A component that answers `false` here fails config load outright, with the same message a
  /// TypeScript rule missing its handler gets: a rule that can never report is a misconfigured
  /// rule, not a quiet no-op.
  export has-check: func(rule: u32) -> bool;
  /// Whether this rule has a cross-file pass, on the same terms.
  ///
  /// Every component exports all seven functions regardless of which passes its rules use,
  /// because a WIT world has no optional exports. A check-only rule's `reduce` is a no-op its
  /// own language's scaffolding supplies.
  export has-reduce: func(rule: u32) -> bool;

  /// Run one rule's per-file pass for one query match.
  ///
  /// `borrow<check-context>`: the host owns the context and lends it for the call, so the
  /// handle is dead the moment `check` returns and cannot be stashed for a later one.
  ///
  /// The `result` is the graceful-failure channel, and it is not a second way of spelling a
  /// trap. A guest that traps loses everything it knew — measured 2026-08-10, an uncaught
  /// JavaScript throw arrives as `wasm trap: unreachable` with no message, no type and no
  /// stack — so a rule that can catch its own failure has to be able to hand one back, or the
  /// host has nothing to remap through the component's source map and nothing to name in a
  /// diagnostic. A Rust guest that panics still traps, and the host still handles it; this
  /// adds a way to fail gracefully and removes nothing.
  export check: func(rule: u32, ctx: borrow<check-context>, m: match) -> result<_, rule-error>;
  /// Run one rule's cross-file pass, once per run, on the same terms as `check`.
  export reduce: func(rule: u32, ctx: borrow<reduce-context>) -> result<_, rule-error>;
}
